For a thousand years, a fleeting fame, a guest of the Golden Horse Gate;
One night of deep drunkenness in the Stone City tower.
Let the men of Ying no longer sing the "Spring in Yangchun" tune;
It has turned white the head of the governor of Hunan.

Mulberry leaves spread thick, shutting out the sun's bright rays;
King Mu invites to a feast where flowing clouds turn to wine.
Rainbow banners touch the ground as clouds first come to rest;
Golden music lifts the sky as the moon begins to slant.
Songs choke in the fine wind that blows the powdered stamens;
After drinking, clear dew dampens the jade-white sand.
Unaware the white horse's red reins have come untied,
It sneaks a bite of the jade-green flowers in the eastern field.

Once I lived near the Peak of Great White Star,
And counted my way to the Temple of Immortals.
When the Black Water's clear, the pool's bottom appears;
Where white clouds break, the cave gate opens.
In the woods, warming wine, I burn red leaves;
On the rock, writing poems, I sweep green moss.
Sadly, those old travels can never be revisited;
In chrysanthemum season, I envy your return.

The duckweed stretches on for more than ten acres wide,
Its beauty has always been shared with people by its side.
The water birds splash now and then, startling my dreams;
The town's moon, dim and hazy, paints a picture, it seems.
Don't go pick orchids on the isle to make a waistband;
Instead, plant orange trees on the shore as servants at hand.
From now on, with my cane and shoes, I'll come and go here;
Please give this place a fine name: Wild Goose Lake so dear.

When the wind shakes the ripe fruit, I hear them fall;
When the rain drips on the remaining flowers, they still smell sweet.
Under the leaves, the yellow-mouthed birds leave the nest and make a clamor;
Among the waves, the small fish chase in schools, busy.

His loyal bones buried deep, his grudge will never cease;
Cold mist locks up the grief of the old mountain still.
The sad wind sweeps away the comet of the day;
His courage could devour the autumn of the land.
He died one day, his heart still served his country;
His fame praised through the ages, his head crowned with jade.
The white-feathered fan upon the cloud back then—
Might it still be allowed to come and be seen again?

When autumn comes, my feelings grow even harder to express;
I lodge in a five-acre garden south of the town.
Dropping the reins to view the hills, I have no iron otter;
Gathering firewood to brew tea, I have a blue ape.
Night rain on the pond adds a new rise;
Mist and weeds on the plains reduce old traces.
Is it that in all my life I have few relatives and friends?
There is scarcely anyone willing to visit this lonely village.

By the green window, the cool mat is only good for sleep;
By the dew-wet well, no dust, shaded by green locust trees.
In dreams I enter the land of drunkenness, still thirsty as if ill;
The sound of the windlass comes to the pillow's edge.

His house overlooks the isle where Yin Xian sent his letter;
His gate is thronged by those who ask after Vimalakirti's health.
The imperial envoy moors his boat, surely to seek the Way;
Rain and flowers cross their shadows beyond the western ford.

My face is covered with sand, my hair with dust;
My eyebrows have lost their paint, my cheeks their bloom.
Sorrow, toil and hardship have worn me out;
Now I look like a picture of despair.

Sick and bony, I look like a mountain recluse;
Surely travelers will laugh at my form and face.
My body, when written down, is much like a coconut;
Enduring troubles, my goiter grows like a gourd.
With money, fine wine can still be obtained;
But who can summon a lofty man with a note?
Better to sweep clean the thatched studio floor,
And lie watching faint incense rise from the tile stove.

A cornered beast still fights strong, its sword not yet stained red;
The youth is ashamed to face the white clover in the wind.
His heart grows cold—the vast land of China seems far away;
His spirit enters the desert depths of the barbarian court.
Who says A-Meng is still in Luoyang?
It is not fitting that old Liang should remain in Longzhong.
In recent years, bow and arrow have become utterly useless;
He can only go to the riverbank to shoot at the evening geese.

On the sea in late autumn the white sun is dim;
The frosty gale whistles and invades limb by limb.
I hold yellow chrysanthemums with Jiangnan in mind;
My eyes follow wild geese a thousand miles behind.
I often think of pure talk, truly splendid and bright;
Who would play "Flowing Water" when few know its delight?
The Dongshan brothers are not hard to follow still;
Who dares be the first to chant a recluse's will?

West of the beacon tower stands a hundred-foot high tower;
At dusk I mount it alone in autumn sea breeze.
A flute plays "The Moon over the Mountain Pass" once more;
Who knows my golden bower's grief for him far overseas?

They did not say the moon would seize the Hu;
A baleful star invaded the general's camp.
At Yiwu, in vain there was resolve;
By the Wei River, the fields could not be plowed.
Many opinions grind away the jade's flaws;
Another year, we will hear the sound of drums.
What harm is there in building a lofty tomb,
To stand as a symbol of Shunchang City?

Ashamed, how can I face the guest star?
Move the boat, do not approach the front shore.
On gull sand, grass grows long, linking river's dark;
At crab sheds, the tide returns, carrying rain's fishy scent.
Returning, still have the first cocoon and chestnut;
Since birth, how many bamboo fish baskets can one use?
My poetic guts in one night sprout sharp angles;
I ask you, old friend, about the pair of jade bottles.

The seas dry up, the dragon departs, rain falls endlessly;
The Jie drum clamors loud through the mountain's forbidden woods.
Tiger warriors drop their bows, their strength is spent;
The rooster-men cease their arrows, the water-clock's sound sinks deep.
Orioles shake the imperial willows—spring still clamors on;
Swallows kick the palace flowers—mist turns ever deeper.
Heartbroken, the traveler in the Kapok Hut;
Pitiful, Mei Wu built up with gold.

The surging waves stretch boundless, eastward their torrents flow.
No measure can contain the hundred streams' vast sweep;
The three isles fade in distance, lost in mystery deep.
The sky is pale, the ancient shrine stands lone;
Clouds hang low where the traveler's path has gone.
I pause, then turn, too weary to look back;
My homeward reins chase the west wind's track.

At dawn, the rain streaks look like hanging ropes;
At dusk, broad waves submerge the fishing shoal.
The ducks return, and all is still and quiet;
The crows fly off, the grass is green and fresh.

My little study gets a new name;
The clear breeze creates its own sound.
Worldly burdens have been cast off;
As yang returns, the way grows long.

Mist veils the sparse willows, a fuzzy green;
Apricot cheeks and peach blooms greet the eye with red.
By chance I met you when snowflakes were seen;
Now I drift alone as spring winds have fled.
Vain grief—the wheels roll far, your voice fades away;
I send my parting heart in laughter and song.
Tonight the moon is bright, all men are silent;
How can I share a cup with longing so strong?

West bank in shade, trees meet the view;
East shore at dusk hides fence from sight.
The moon sets, sand along the stream flows;
Stars fly, willows on the bank move.
Bright fireflies haunt the shallow grass;
A hidden bird picks a deep branch.
Sitting at midnight, back to the water,
Too much sorrow, no illness can bear.

The relics of the Six Dynasties remain here in vain;
The town o'erlooks blue waves up to the sea gate.
The boundless land and streams come into drunken eyes;
Autumn scene from the sky enters the poet's soul.
Today we hear the song of jade trees sad and drear;
The royal flags of bygone days no longer cheer.
The same are scenes, but different men we meet today.
How can I not be sad with a cup of wine gay!

A monk's dwelling is lonely, unlike an official's abode;
Seeking lines, searching guts, twisting and breaking whiskers.
How much more in the rain, thinking of what matters;
The dream-soul returns to the corner of the planting hills.

The waterclock runs out, the night is deep and still;
In the mountain lodge wind and dew are chill.
No cloud arises on the ridge's crest;
The moon sinks in the wave's heart to rest.

The Lady of the Southern Peak drinks spring wine so green;
Lately it can't compare to jade cream's mellow sheen.
Mu Sheng won't be forced, though he might sip a while;
Some day on Yingzhou Isle, old friends we'll be.

On Pinggai Mountain high, ancient trees are sparse;
Here long ago someone built an immortal's dwelling.
The well links eight corners, cold springs gush forth;
The temple burned, Three Purities left after kalpa fire.
By the ink-washing pool, the cinnabar stove is cold;
Beside Dragon-Tethering Cliff, the stone pool lies empty.
Having finished my chant, I sit alone on barren moss,
Idly watching a snail learn seal-script writing.
("Records of Famous Sites in Shu," Vol. 12, "Pengshan County, Meizhou")

On a frosty clear day, I suddenly think of the riverbank;
My weak donkey and thin boy, the road already dry.
How could my bony frame bear wealth and honor?
I only rejoice my temple hair endures grief and joy.
On a traveler's road, winter warm, my fur robe thin;
In a village market, a good year, wine cups generous.
Of the lingering twelfth month, only ten days remain;
I'll seek water dropwort and smartweed to grace the spring plate.

The marshal, humble and courteous, fully understands the feelings of those below;
In the Hall of Unicorns, as evening falls, he further invites guests.
All who meet the wind are fellow travelers in the same boat;
How could one treat the people of Qin as strangers to those of Yue?

Do not begrudge spending wine money when morning comes;
Yuanming himself is but a flower immortal.
On Double Ninth, the cup brims full and overflows;
A single thread of golden bloom is one whole year.

Cold clouds press in on every side, the traveler's days grow short;
In a vast field, a cracked wine jar lies.
Your family's great gate, my father's faction,
Seeking white brew, we visit back and forth.
Blending incense, we ask for many ingredients, armor-plated;
Deep orchids, several times played, we tend the lone paulownia.
Since then, our baggage has wandered north and south,
Jaggedly, we do not see the peaks among the clouds.
Climbing mountains, facing waters, there is no lack of longing;
Alone laughing, often we stroke the tall pines.
Thatched gate, late spring, swallows' voices are lovely;
By the book curtain, winding in, comes clear wind.
The five storehouses, stained with dust, are washed entirely clean;
A thousand miles of ancient color opens the sorrowful breast.
Where is Wangchuan? Guangwen is dead;
One after another, youngsters boast of their achievements.
The man of Chu barely escaped two days of suffering,
Yet sighs that white jade is buried in mugwort and weeds.
Hesitating, we take wine to recount our separation,
A hundred rivers rush to drink, startling the long rainbow.
Elegance has not yet shamed the days after Zhengshi;
Fallen and lost, Qiu and Fan are judged without a bell.
Your talent, if used, is not only this;
I too will soon become an old man.
A fine steed, once it finds the road, goes a thousand li;
We shall watch, wiping our eyes, the swan at the sky's edge.

At dawn I roam Lion Peak, at dusk Yanghua;
I love the water and hills, my joy knows no bound.
The crane and gull are used to seeing visitors;
A pair of them stand on the sand by the ferry.

Half a pole of spring flood greens the stream level and wide,
In the second month, riverside grass grows far and wide.
Beyond a thousand acres, boats like mayflies appear;
A single branch shelters a bird that mocks the spear.
By the wild bridge, willow threads sway in soft slanting breeze;
At the curved rail, flowers glow in low evening light.
I wonder why the pearl-seeker has not come near;
With clear wine and painted boat, I ask for verse to write.
